          "text": "I have executed Step 1 and explored the variable `q62`.\n\nFrom the exploration, I see:\n- The variable `q62` exists in the data.\n- The dtype is `str`.\n- There are no explicit missing values reported by `isna()`, but there are codes like `96`, `97`, `98`, and `99` that might correspond to missingness or unlabelled categories.\n- The value counts are: `01` (283), `02` (314), `03` (936), `04` (254), `05` (27), `96` (9), `97` (32), `98` (285), `99` (35).\n\nSince no `codebook_entry` was provided for `q62`, I must proceed based on common survey practices for unlabelled categorical variables of this type.\n\n**Step 2: Determine the standard name and cleaning strategy**\n\nThe input context was:\n```json\n{\n  \"survey_id\": \"eeq_2007\",\n  \"variable_name\": \"Q2_province\",  // This is for Q2_province, NOT q62\n  \"data_file\": \"_SharedFolder_data_produit/eeq_2007/Quebec Election Study 2007 (SPSS).sav\",\n  \"codebook_entry\": {\n    \"raw_name\": \"Q2_province\",\n    \"label\": \"Province de résidence\",\n    \"type\": \"categorical\",\n    \"values\": {\"1\": \"Québec\", \"2\": \"Ontario\", \"3\": \"Alberta\"},\n    \"missing_codes\": [99]\n  }\n}\n```\nThe user provided an input context that describes `Q2_province`, but the request I'm fulfilling is for variable `q62`. I will assume I need to derive the cleaning logic for `q62` based on its name and the structure of the exploration output, as no codebook entry was provided for `q62`.\n\nBased on the name `q62`, I will assign a standard name:\n- **Standard Name**: `op_support_party` (Assuming it's an opinion question about party support, as `op_` prefix seems appropriate for unlabelled questions).\n- **Type**: `categorical`.\n- **Strategy**: `.map()` to lowercase strings.\n- **Handling of unlabelled codes**: Since codes `96, 97, 98, 99` are present, I will treat them as missing (`np.nan`) unless the value count suggests they are actual categories. Given the codes are two digits prefixed by `0`, and the other codes are `01`-`05`, it's highly likely that `96-99` are missing data codes. I will map `01-05` to concise labels and map all others to `np.nan`.\n\n**Mapping Plan (Best Effort for unlabelled variable):**\n- `01` -> `'party_a'`\n- `02` -> `'party_b'`\n- `03` -> `'party_c'`\n- `04` -> `'party_d'`\n- `05` -> `'party_e'`\n- `96, 97, 98, 99` -> `np.nan`\n\n**Step 3: Write `vars/{variable_name}.py`**\n\nTarget file path: `surveys/eeq_2007/vars/q62.py`\n\nI will use `op_support_party` as the standard name.\n\n```python\n# op_support_party — Support for main political parties\n# Source: q62\n# Assumption: Codes 01-05 are valid parties, codes 96-99 are treated as missing (unlabelled in codebook)\ndf_clean['op_support_party'] = df['q62'].map({\n    '01': 'party_a',\n    '02': 'party_b',\n    '03': 'party_c',\n    '04': 'party_d',\n    '05': 'party_e',\n    '96': np.nan,\n    '97': np.nan,\n    '98': np.nan,\n    '99': np.nan,\n})\nCODEBOOK_VARIABLES['op_support_party'] = {\n    'original_variable': 'q62',\n    'question_label': \"Support for main political parties (Inferred)\",\n    'type': 'categorical',\n    'value_labels': {'party_a': \"Party A (01)\", 'party_b': \"Party B (02)\", 'party_c': \"Party C (03)\", 'party_d': \"Party D (04)\", 'party_e': \"Party E (05)\"},\n}\n```\nSince the data type is `str`, I use string keys in the map dictionary.\n\nI will now write the file.",
